        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-qft-x03-photon-count-pvm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Then $N$ is self-adjoint on its stated domain and $E_N$ is a projection-valued measure",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "They obey $$W(h)W(k)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\operatorname{Im}\\langle h,k\\rangle_2\\right)W(h+k),\\qquad \\langle\\Omega,W(h)\\Omega\\rangle_2=e^{-\\|h\\|^2/4},$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "thm-qft-example-soft-current-angular-coherent",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "This is a conditional mathematical theorem about a prescribed massive\ncharge-flow current and an exact free coherent-state model. The transverse\nphoton-space carrier [F2] and coherent-tail carrier [L1] are present at their\ndisplayed interfaces. The coherent-tail proof now declares its exact\npolar-coordinate and Euclidean surface-measure suppliers. Its weak-zero and\nno-strong-limit conclusions are used under the hypotheses of [L1]. That\nStatement contains no projective-ray conclusion; this item derives the ray\nconclusion separately from the exact coherent overlap in step 7.1. Local\nsource repairs do not confer independent acceptance or replace current\nnative readiness reconciliation.\n\nAssume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the constants and conventions of\n[[def-qft-example-data]], and that the two current supplier interfaces [F2]\nand [L1] hold exactly as stated below. Let $I$ be a finite, possibly empty,\nset of legs with energies $E_i>0$ in joules, dimensionless velocities\n$v_i=\\mathbf v_{i,\\rm phys}/c\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with $|v_i|<1$, dimensionless charges $Q_i\\in\\mathbb R$,\nsigns $\\eta_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, and charge balance\n$\\sum_{i\\in I}\\eta_iQ_i=0$. Use $h=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ for\nalgebraic four-vector contractions. For $n\\in S^2$ and energy\n$\\omega>0$ in joules, put\n$$ p_i=E_i(1,v_i),\\qquad q=\\omega(1,n),\\qquad K(n)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\eta_iQ_i\\frac{(1,v_i)}{1-v_i\\cdot n}. $$\nDefine the prescribed current\n$J(q)=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\eta_iQ_i\\,p_i/h(p_i,q)$. Since\n$h(p_i,q)=E_i\\omega(1-v_i\\cdot n)>0$, this equals $K(n)/\\omega$.\nLet $P_T(n)=I-n\\otimes n$ and\n$K_\\perp(n)=P_T(n)\\mathbf K(n)$. Define $d\\Omega$ as Euclidean chart\nsurface measure on $S^2$ and\n$$ C=\\frac{g^2}{2(2\\pi)^3}\\int_{S^2}|K_\\perp(n)|^2\\,d\\Omega(n)\\ge0, \\qquad h_\\lambda(q)=g\\frac{K_\\perp(n)}{\\omega} \\mathbf1_{\\{\\lambda<\\omega<\\Lambda\\}}, \\quad 0<\\lambda<\\Lambda, $$\nwhere $g>0$ is the dimensionless coupling of [[def-qft-example-data]] and\n$\\lambda,\\Lambda$ are energy cutoffs in joules. In the energy-coordinate\ntransverse Hilbert space\n$L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3q/((2\\pi)^3\\,2|q|);\\mathbb C^3)$, the exact norm is\n$$ N_\\lambda=\\|h_\\lambda\\|^2=C\\log(\\Lambda/\\lambda). $$\nThe energy-coordinate wavefunction has units $\\mathrm J^{-1}$ and its\none-particle measure has units $\\mathrm J^2$, so $N_\\lambda$ and $C$ are\ndimensionless; its value at $q=0$ is immaterial to this $L^2$ vector.\nUnder the current canonical isometry to the length-momentum photon space,\nthe same vector is\n$$ f_\\lambda(k)=\\mathbf1_{\\{\\lambda/(\\hbar c)<|k|<\\Lambda/(\\hbar c)\\}} |k|^{-3/2}b(k/|k|),\\qquad b(n)=\\frac{gK_\\perp(n)}{\\sqrt{2(2\\pi)^3}},\\qquad \\int_{S^2}|b|^2\\,d\\Omega=C. $$\nThe normalized coherent vector\n$\\Psi_\\lambda=W(-i\\sqrt2 f_\\lambda)\\Omega\n=e^{-N_\\lambda/2}\\epsilon(f_\\lambda)$ has squared norm in sector $m$\n$$ s_m=e^{-N_\\lambda}\\frac{N_\\lambda^m}{m!},\\qquad \\sum_{m\\ge0}s_m=1,\\qquad \\sum_{m\\ge0}m s_m=N_\\lambda. $$\nThese are Hilbert-space sector norms and their index sum; no measured\nprobability is asserted. For the free photon energy operator\n$H_\\gamma=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(|k|)$, $\\Psi_\\lambda$ is in its operator domain\nand its quadratic expectation is $C(\\Lambda-\\lambda)$ joules.\n\nIf $C=0$, $f_\\lambda=0$ almost everywhere and $\\Psi_\\lambda=\\Omega$ for each\ncutoff. If $C>0$, the exact hypotheses of the current [L1] hold with\n$\\epsilon=\\lambda/(\\hbar c)$, $\\kappa=\\Lambda/(\\hbar c)$ and the displayed\nnonzero transverse angular function $b$. Conditional on [L1], the family has\nno strong Fock-vector limit and converges weakly to zero, while its free\nenergy expectation stays bounded. Step 7.1 proves separately that its rays\nhave no projective limit, including after arbitrary phase choices. This is\nnot a result about full interacting QED, universal charged sectors,\ndetector-inclusive observables, or measured photon statistics.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1",
            "4.2",
            "4.3",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each $i$ and $\\mathbf n\\in S^2$, $p_i\\cdot q=\\omega E_i(1-\\mathbf v_i\\cdot\\mathbf n) \\ge\\omega E_i(1-|\\mathbf v_i|)>0$. Since there are finitely many massive terms, $K^\\mu(\\mathbf n)=\\sum_i s_iQ_i(1,\\mathbf v_i)/(1-\\mathbf v_i\\cdot\\mathbf n)$; each denominator is bounded below by $1-|\\mathbf v_i|>0$, so $K$ and its transverse components are bounded. For the measurable orthonormal frame $(\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2,\\mathbf n)$, $\\sum_r|\\mathbf K\\cdot\\epsilon_r|^2 =|\\mathbf K-(\\mathbf K\\cdot\\mathbf n)\\mathbf n|^2=|K_\\perp|^2$. Thus the integrand is nonnegative and measurable, and compactness of $S^2$ gives $0\\le C<\\infty$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-2-1",
          "claim": "For the one-charge rest-to-velocity current, put $\\mu=\\mathbf n\\cdot\\hat{\\mathbf z}$. Its spatial current is $\\mathbf K=v\\hat{\\mathbf z}/(1-v\\mu)$, hence $|K_\\perp|^2=v^2(1-\\mu^2)/(1-v\\mu)^2$. Integrating over azimuth and then setting $x=1-v\\mu$ gives $$I(v):=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\frac{v^2(1-\\mu^2)}{(1-v\\mu)^2}\\,d\\mu =\\frac1v\\int_{1-v}^{1+v} \\left(\\frac{v^2-1}{x^2}+\\frac2x-1\\right)dx =\\frac1v\\left[\\frac{1-v^2}{x}+2\\log x-x\\right]_{1-v}^{1+v} =\\frac2v\\log\\frac{1+v}{1-v}-4.$$ Therefore $$C(v)=\\frac{g_{\\mathrm{em}}^2}{2(2\\pi)^3}(2\\pi I(v)) =\\frac{\\alpha}{\\pi} \\left(\\frac1v\\log\\frac{1+v}{1-v}-2\\right).$$ For $f(v)=\\log((1+v)/(1-v))-2v$, $f(0)=0$ and $f'(v)=2v^2/(1-v^2)>0$ on $(0,1)$, so $C(v)>0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-2-2",
          "claim": "The fixed-current, charge-balance, transverse-polarization, and cutoff hypotheses match [L1]. The current formula simplifies to $J(q)=K(\\mathbf n)/\\omega$, and step 1.1 identifies the polarization sum with $|K_\\perp|^2$, exactly the angular convention of [L1]. This is a conditional use of the inspected draft carrier at its actual interface.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-3-1",
          "claim": "The proved norm and unitary-map arguments of [L1] give $\\|h_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}\\|^2=N_\\gamma$ and the displayed unitary $U_E$ into $\\mathfrak h_T$. Its coherent-sector formula supplies the normalized vector $\\Psi_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}$ in the Statement. These uses require only the actual mathematical inputs of the inspected draft carrier; its separate Q8-dependent infrared clause is not used.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "L1",
            "step 2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-4-1",
          "claim": "By [F5]–[F6], $P_n$ is an orthogonal projection and $I-P_n$ is its orthogonal complement. The two Kraus operators $P_n$ and $I-P_n$ are bounded and satisfy $P_n^*P_n+(I-P_n)^*(I-P_n)=P_n+(I-P_n)=I$. They therefore form a normalized finite projective instrument. The state from step 3.1 is normalized, so [A3] assigns the model probability $p_n=\\|P_n\\Psi_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}\\|^2$ to its first outcome. This uses a finite two-outcome partition for each singleton event and assumes no countably infinite Kraus instrument.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A3",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-4-2",
          "claim": "The expected photon energy is the energy-coordinate expectation from [L1], transferred to the adopted free Hamiltonian $H_\\gamma$ by its unitary map and [A2]. The cutoffs are energies in joules, so $C(\\Lambda-\\lambda)$ has unit joule.",
          "step": "4.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A2",
            "L1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-4-3",
          "claim": "Let $\\lambda_j=\\Lambda e^{-j}$ for $j\\ge1$, and set $u_j=U_Eh_{\\lambda_j,\\Lambda}$ and $V_j=W(-i\\sqrt2u_j)$. The difference $u_{j+1}-u_j$ is the same current amplitude restricted to the shell $\\lambda_{j+1}<\\omega<\\lambda_j$. Applying [L1]'s cutoff norm formula to that shell gives $\\|u_{j+1}-u_j\\|^2=C\\log(\\lambda_j/\\lambda_{j+1})=C$. By [L1], $\\Psi_{\\lambda_j,\\Lambda}=V_j\\Omega$. Unitarity and the Weyl product law in [F7] give $V_j^*V_{j+1}=e^{i\\theta_j}W(-i\\sqrt2(u_{j+1}-u_j))$ for some real phase $\\theta_j$. Applying the vacuum expectation formula in [F7] yields $$|\\langle\\Psi_{\\lambda_j,\\Lambda},\\Psi_{\\lambda_{j+1},\\Lambda}\\rangle_2| =e^{-\\|u_{j+1}-u_j\\|^2/2}=e^{-C/2}<1.$$",
          "step": "4.3",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "L1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-5-1",
          "claim": "The projection $P_n$ retains exactly sector $n$ by [F5]. The sector formula from [L1] gives $P_n\\Psi_{\\lambda,\\Lambda} =e^{-N_\\gamma/2}(U_Eh_{\\lambda,\\Lambda})^{\\otimes_s n}/\\sqrt{n!}$. The symmetric tensor norm is multiplicative and $U_E$ preserves the one-particle norm, so $p_n=e^{-N_\\gamma}N_\\gamma^n/n!$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "L1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-6-1",
          "claim": "The exponential series gives $\\sum_{n\\ge0}p_n=e^{-N_\\gamma}e^{N_\\gamma}=1$ and, by reindexing its nonnegative terms, $\\sum_{n\\ge0}n p_n=e^{-N_\\gamma}N_\\gamma e^{N_\\gamma}=N_\\gamma$. The zero-sector term is $e^{-N_\\gamma}$, including $N_\\gamma=0$ under $0^0=1$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "pthm-qft-example-soft-coherent-counts-step-7-1",
          "claim": "For unit vectors $x,y$, expansion of $\\|x-\\zeta y\\|^2$ and choice of $\\zeta$ to align their inner product give $\\inf_{|\\zeta|=1}\\|x-\\zeta y\\|^2=2-2|\\langle x,y\\rangle_2|$. Step 4.3 therefore gives the fixed positive adjacent-ray distance $2(1-e^{-C/2})$ along $\\lambda_j$. A projective-ray limit would make these adjacent distances tend to zero, a contradiction. Ordinary Fock-norm convergence is the special case without phase adjustment and is also impossible. The energy from step 4.2 tends to the finite value $C\\Lambda$; nonconvergence is not caused by divergent mean energy.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.3",
            "step 4.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [
